>* For standard maze levels, introduce probabilities that make the
>  down stairs more likely to be somewhat near the up stairs on
>  most levels, so that the average length of the path between them,
>  once the levels are fully explored, is shorter in most games.
>  Nothing drastic that would allow the player to locate the next
>  stairs by just searching the immediate vicinity of the other ones;
>  just tip the scales a little in favor of somewhat shorter paths
>  between them on average.  Maybe something like, after initial
>  placement, calculate the distance between the stairs and if
>  rand(calculated_distance) > rand(5)*rand(6) then pick new random
>  locations for the stairs.


What if the distance between up and down stairs was roughly
proportional to some *current* formula, perhaps involving current
luck, and therefore that distance could *change* on every re-visit to
a level?

In other words, allow staircase locations to move.  It may not make a
lot of sense physically, unless we say that staircases are more like
magic portals between levels with somewhat unspecific destinations.

On the other hand, allowing this flexibility could add some new
uncertainty and danger in travelling between levels.  The idea could
also be expanded to allow re-visited levels to be slightly remodeled
in other strategic ways.

